VERA20k @VERSION@ (build @BUILD@, early alpha)

VERA20k is a free, open-source reimplementation of Command & Conquer: Yuri's
Revenge. It contains no game files: you need your own copy of Yuri's Revenge
1.001, for example from The Ultimate Collection on Steam or the EA app.

GETTING STARTED
On Windows, the installer sets everything up: it wrote config.toml in the
install folder, where VERA20k also keeps its settings, logs, saves and
replays. To use another game folder, run the installer again.

On macOS, drag VERA20k from the disk image into Applications and open it (the
first time, see REQUIREMENTS). It looks for Yuri's Revenge in Porting Kit,
Wineskin and CrossOver installs and offers the one it finds, or asks you to
choose the folder. It keeps config.toml, its settings, logs, saves and replays
in ~/Library/Application Support/VERA20k (in Finder, choose Go > Go to
Folder). To use another game folder, change ra2_dir in that config.toml.

On Linux, make the AppImage executable (chmod +x, or Properties in your file
manager) and open it. It looks for Yuri's Revenge in Steam libraries and in
Wine prefixes (~/.wine, Lutris's ~/Games, Bottles, PlayOnLinux and CrossOver)
and offers the one it finds, or asks you to choose the folder; it asks with
zenity or kdialog. It keeps config.toml, its settings, logs, saves and
replays in ~/.local/share/VERA20k. To use another game folder, change ra2_dir
in that config.toml.

REQUIREMENTS
  A GPU with Vulkan, DirectX 12 or Metal.
  Windows: VERA20k is not code-signed yet. SmartScreen may warn about an
    unknown app; choose More info, then Run anyway. Smart App Control, which
    some Windows 11 PCs have on, blocks unsigned programs outright: there,
    VERA20k runs only with it turned off, in Windows Security > App & browser
    control > Smart App Control settings.
  macOS: VERA20k is not notarized by Apple yet, so macOS blocks it the first
    time you open it. Click Done, open System Settings > Privacy & Security,
    scroll down, click Open Anyway, enter your password and confirm. The
    button stays for about an hour after the block; if it is gone, open
    VERA20k again. Each new version asks once.
  Linux: needs ALSA (libasound2), a Vulkan driver, and libxkbcommon-x11-0
    for X11 keyboard support. The AppImage mounts itself with FUSE (fusermount, from the fuse3 or fuse package), which most
    desktops have; without it, start it with --appimage-extract-and-run.

This is early software: expect missing features and differences from the
original game. Report them at https://github.com/YuriPlanet/vera20k/issues

Official downloads come only from
https://github.com/YuriPlanet/vera20k/releases. To check that an installer was
built there, run: gh attestation verify <installer> --repo YuriPlanet/vera20k

